INT. SLEEP CLINIC - OBSERVATION ROOM - NIGHT

A dim control room. A bank of monitors shows a bedroom set on the other side of a glass wall: a narrow bed, a nightstand, a patient in sensor wires. Green EEG traces scroll across the screens.

DR. HORTENSE ADEWALE-SUMNER (50s, cardigan, reading glasses) sits at the console with a pencil behind her ear. Beside her, TECHNICIAN BARNABAS QUILL-MENDOZA (20s, scrubs, a tiny plant on his desk) watches the traces with his chin on his hand.

HORTENSE
Delta waves are steady.

BARNABAS
He's gone deep.

HORTENSE
Since eleven.

She sips tea. Sets the cup down. Writes a note. Barnabas shifts in his chair and swivels half-turn toward her.

BARNABAS
Doctor, may I ask something?

HORTENSE
You may ask.

BARNABAS
Does anyone ever really sleep in here?

HORTENSE
Everyone sleeps. That's the problem.

On the monitor, the sleeping patient rolls over. A wire tugs loose. The trace stutters, flattens, and spikes. Hortense and Barnabas lean forward at the same moment, shoulders bumping.

HORTENSE (CONT'D)
Check the electrode.

BARNABAS
Left temple.

He stands. She stands. They both reach for the door at the same time, stop, and she gestures for him to go first. He does. She hesitates, watching the monitor, then follows.

Through the glass, Barnabas crosses the bedroom, adjusts the wire, and smooths the sleeper's hair. Hortense stays in the doorway with her arms folded. The patient murmurs something. The two of them freeze, then look at each other.

BARNABAS
(whisper)
What did he say?

HORTENSE
(whisper)
Something about a window.

They wait. The sleeper's breathing lengthens. Barnabas backs away on tiptoe, brushes the plant, and catches it before it falls.

HORTENSE (CONT'D)
(whisper)
Good catch.

BARNABAS
(whisper)
Thanks.

They return to the console. The traces settle into long green waves. Hortense picks up the tea, finds it cold, and drinks it anyway.
